    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nCython 3.3 generates unbalanced memoryview acquisition bookkeeping when two attributes of an extension class are assigned different slices of the same typed memoryview.\n\nWhen the extension-class instance is destroyed, generated cleanup code aborts the interpreter while clearing the second attribute:\n\n```text\nFatal Python error: __pyx_fatalerror: Acquisition count is -1\nPython runtime state: initialized\n```\n\nThe reproducer below succeeds with Cython 3.2.9 but fails with both Cython 3.3.0 and the current `master` branch. It does not contain manual deallocation, raw pointers, `nogil` code, or explicit manipulation of memoryview ownership.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n`repro.pyx`:\n\n```cython\n# cython: language_level=3\n\ncdef class TwoSlices:\n    cdef const double[:, ::1] first\n    cdef const double[:, ::1] second\n\n    def set_slices(self, const double[:, ::1] data):\n        self.first = data[:, :1]\n        self.second = data[:, 1:]\n\n\ndef reproduce():\n    import numpy as np\n\n    value = TwoSlices()\n    value.set_slices(np.ones((2, 2)))\n```\n\n`setup.py`:\n\n```python\nfrom Cython.Build import cythonize\nfrom setuptools import Extension, setup\n\nimport numpy as np\n\n\nsetup(\n    ext_modules=cythonize(\n        [Extension(\"repro\", [\"repro.pyx\"], include_dirs=[np.get_include()])],\n        language_level=3,\n    )\n)\n```\n\nBuild and run:\n\n```console\npython setup.py build_ext --inplace\npython -c \"import repro; repro.reproduce()\"\n```\n\nWith Cython 3.3.0 or current `master`, the process aborts as the local `TwoSlices` instance is destroyed:\n\n```text\nFatal Python error: __pyx_fatalerror: Acquisition count is -1\n```\n\nThe same source builds and exits normally with Cython 3.2.9.\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nAssigning two slices of the same input memoryview to two extension-class attributes should produce balanced acquisition bookkeeping. Destroying the instance should not abort the interpreter.\n\n### OS\n\nWindows NT 10.0.26100.0\n\n### Python version\n\nPython 3.10.11\n\n### Cython version\n\n- 3.2.9: passes\n- 3.3.0: fails\n- `master` at `418acffa8c56b1b8767ab9993a5cdfb81d9e766f`: fails\n\n### Additional context\n\nThis may be related to cython/cython#5507 and its implementation in cython/cython#6435, which was included in the Cython 3.3 milestone. That change optimizes memoryview-slice assignment by skipping reference-count operations when the left- and right-hand sides share the same underlying memoryview.\n\nThe two assignments in this reproducer satisfy that condition: both attributes receive slices backed by the same input memoryview. The generated destructor later clears both attributes, and clearing the second one takes the acquisition count to `-1`.\n\nThere are older reports of similar acquisition-count asymmetries in other generated-code paths:\n\n- cython/cython#5571 — duplicate or incorrect cleanup of memoryview arguments to special functions\n- cython/cython#4662 — under-counted memoryview temporaries in loops\n- cython/cython#4798 — under-counted closure-captured memoryview arguments\n\nHowever, I could not find an existing report covering multiple extension-class memoryview attributes assigned slices of the same backing array.",
